-- title:
--   Rational cyclicity of the inertia-displacement Tate module at 2
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime and let $B$ be a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with $2$ a non-unit of $B$ (the hypothesis `B.LiesOverPrime 2`), so that $B$ determines a place above $2$; let $I_B \subseteq \operatorname{Gal}(\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q)$ be the image of its inertia subgroup under the inclusion of the decomposition subgroup. The group $J =$ `JZero p` is the degree-zero divisor class group of the level-$p$ modular function field over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$, equipped with the Galois action and with the module structure `heckeModuleBar p` over `HeckeAlg` $= \mathbb Z[X_\ell : \ell \text{ prime}]$. For $M \in \mathbb N$, `eisensteinTorsionBar p 2 M` is the subgroup of elements of $J$ killed by the $M$-th power of the ideal $\mathfrak P$ obtained by pulling back $(2) \subseteq \mathbb Z$ along `eisensteinEval p`. Write $D \subseteq J$ for the subgroup generated by all $\sigma x - x$ with $\sigma \in I_B$ and $x$ killed by some $\mathfrak P^M$, and let $T =$ [`TateModule 2 (JZero p)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) be the group of sequences $(x_n)_{n \in \mathbb N}$ in $J$ with $2^n x_n = 0$ and $2x_{n+1} = x_n$. The assertion is that there is $s_0 \in T$ all of whose components lie in $D$ such that for every $s \in T$ with all components in $D$ there are $c \in \mathbb N$ and an element $\alpha$ of the $\mathbb Z_2$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb Z_2}(T)$ generated by the image of `tateHeckeRep 2 (JZero p)` on `HeckeAlg`, with $2^c \cdot s = \alpha(s_0)$.
--
--   This is the rational cyclicity, over the $2$-adic Hecke algebra, of the Tate module of the inertia-displacement subgroup at $2$ of the Eisenstein $2$-power torsion of $J_0(p)$, in the style of Mazur's study of the Eisenstein ideal. It feeds the subsequent statement [`ModularCurve.exists_generator_tateModule_inf_pi_closure_inertia_smul_sub_and_smul_eisensteinTorsionBar_eq_zero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.exists_generator_tateModule_inf_pi_closure_inertia_smul_sub_and_smul_eisensteinTorsionBar_eq_zero), where the generator is combined with an annihilation condition on the Eisenstein torsion.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_generator_tateModule_closure_inertia_smul_sub_adjoin_tateHeckeRep.lean

